    "liar_game_data": {
        "topic": "The 'Seekhna' Confusion",
        "question": "If you want to say 'I am learning' vs 'I am teaching', you use 'Seekhna' and 'Sikhana'. How do you remember which is which?",
        "options": [
            "They mean the same thing",
            "Seekhna (Short 'i') is learning, Sikhana (Long 'ee') is teaching",
            "Sikhana (सिभोजन) is teaching, Seekhna (सीखना) is learning",
            "One is only for Hindi"
        ],
        "correct_answer_index": 2,
        "explanation": "In Hindi, 'Seekhna' (सीखना) means to learn (self-focused action), while 'Sikhana' (सिभोजन) means to teach (external focus). The causal form changes the meaning entirely!"
    }
